npm install oidc-client  --save

import {UserManager, User} from 'oidc-client';

scope definition: high-level access control identifiers for a backend resource (ex: api endpoint)

login and logout must go to the sts, as sts handles the token expiration.

Wrap the sts interaction within an angular "home-made-authentication" service.
Send config params to the sts, such as: 
* the scopes desired for the app (those scopes must be configured on the sts already)
* an app identifier
* a redirect uri callback to be used when login is valid.
* and more, see => https://app.pluralsight.com/course-player?clipId=3296def2-473e-4498-93b7-d8084a231701

Add functions in the AppComponent that interact with the "home-made-authentication" service to check if the user is logged in or not.

Should download FIDDLER, to look at request and responses for the oidc flow.  Way better than what's in the console.

Example of identity server response with a cookie (to prevent the connection page when already logged in)
Response (302) ---

Location:	http://localhost;4200/signing-callback?code=hjgjgahghjs...
Set-Cookie: .AspNetCore.Identity.Application=CslkjajdsfhHKJ...

_userManager.signinRedirectCallback( user => {} ) // to be placed in "home-made-authentication" service to update the user field.

In Angular, the component "routed" (as configured in app-routing.module.ts) by the "app-signin-callback URI" provided in the 302 emitted by the STS *must* complete the login before giving back control to the end-user.
To do so, ngOnInit() {
	this._authService.completeLogin().then( user => {
		this._router.navigate(['/'], { replaceUrl:true });	// replaceUrl to remove signingRedirectCallbackComponent FROM THE "BACK NAVIGATION" STACK
	});	
 }

Upon authentication, the oidc-client User object contains the id and access bearer tokens.

"home-made-authentication" service logout
called by logout button click handler ==> this._userManager.signoutRedirect();
also called after to complete logout (complete from the Angular app standpoint) ==> this._userManager.signoutRedirectCallback();


